WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.710
We are welcoming back today a longtime friend
of the show, Benjamin Dixon, host of the Benjamin

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:12.110
Dixon Show, resident of the all important
state of Georgia.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:16.630
We are going to talk about what already happened
in Georgia, what may happen on January 5th

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:17.630
then.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.119
It's so good to see you, David, as always.

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:20.119
Good to see you.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:21.590
Good to be back here sharing with you.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:23.340
Congratulations on the million subscribers.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.430
Thank you.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:25.430
Million.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:26.430
One million to now.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.430
Right.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:28.529
Well, listen, you're going to get there faster
than I did it.

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:33.609
You're not there yet, but you're compressing
my growth into less time, I'm sure.

00:00:33.609 --> 00:00:38.340
I certainly hope so, because it already feels
like it's been forever as it's been like six

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.340
years.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.210
So but we're just still doing the work and
excited about what's happening in Georgia.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.210
Yeah.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.900
So, I mean, let's just dove right into it.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.199
First of all, you know, there were questions,
will Texas go red?

00:00:48.199 --> 00:00:49.199
I never.

00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:50.199
I'm sorry.

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:51.199
Well, Texas go blue.

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:52.199
I never thought it would.

00:00:52.199 --> 00:00:53.199
What's going to happen in Florida?

00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:55.179
I thought it was kind of a question mark.

00:00:55.179 --> 00:01:00.520
I did not think Georgia was the most likely
to go to Joe Biden, but it did.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.080
You followed it in a way that that I didn't
from outside of the state.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.300
Were you surprised?

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:06.720
No, I wasn't.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.720
I was pleased.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:08.720
I was thoroughly pleased.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.770
But I knew the kind of work that was happening
on the ground.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:13.690
I knew the type of registrations that were
happening.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.180
New Georgia Project, which is affiliated with
Stacey Abrams and a couple of other colleagues

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.950
of mine, New Georgia Project, registered half
a million people of color alone in this cycle.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.670
And so with those kind of numbers and that
type of targeted push that I saw on the ground,

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.040
I suspected that Georgia would be blue.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.700
I just didn't want to jinx it.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.480
So I kept my fingers crossed.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:42.530
So we've got these two Senate races and John
Joseph, David Perdue, there's no more debates

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:43.910
there.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:48.760
I almost had to call Atlanta police and report
a murder after that first debate with Josephine

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:49.760
Perdue.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.580
I mean, that's one of the most brutal debates
I've ever seen.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.900
And I understand why David Perdue is not debating.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:02:01.030
We had the Lefler Warnaco debate on Sunday
and she seemed to have a software malfunction

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:07.530
where she was repeating the same five things
over and over, including the word socialist

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.200
and a radical leftist radical liberal, Raphael
Warnock.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.590
My instinct in my audience was this is so
transparent, but we don't necessarily understand

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.390
the totality of the electorate in Georgia.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:23.620
It's that type of repetition, actually, something
that could win you a Senate race in Georgia.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.870
No, no.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:30.680
But what can win you a Senate race in Georgia
is simply being complicit and part and parcel

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.310
with the power structure that Kelly Lefler
represents and what she's trying to protect.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.900
So they don't care how incompetent she is.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.900
She's theirs.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.010
She's that that's that's their that's their
senator.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.870
That's the person who they're going to vote
for.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:43.220
I mean, they voted for Donald Trump.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.220
Right?

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.710
I know Joe Biden won, but there's still a
lot of people in Georgia who support Trump

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:48.710
ism.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:52.890
So do they support her malfunction because
she did have a software malfunction, to be

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:53.890
sure?

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.410
No, they probably think she's she's bland
as a Borg, but they're still going to vote

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.540
for her.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:04.880
And in terms of voting at all, and this is
something that I am just fascinated by.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:11.091
On the one hand, early it was it's rigged,
rigged, rigged, Trump really won, etc., which

00:03:11.091 --> 00:03:15.020
would imply January 5th could maybe is also
rigged.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.050
And then you started seeing inklings of this.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.460
Let's just boycott Republicans boycott if
they're not going to count our votes anyway.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.420
Let's just boycott, which, of course, is crazy
because that will just hand the wins to to

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.180
the two Democratic candidates.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:30.710
But lately, in Trump's rally, during the debate,
you saw Lefler say it.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.270
They're now focusing on the importance of
getting out every vote.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.500
Do you think that this boycott movement will
have any impact?

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.810
Well, I will say that the Republicans here
in the state of Georgia are pretty much like

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.349
the Republicans across the country.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:49.570
They they're doing their best to hold it together
while they figure out what the hell to do

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.270
with Donald Trump.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.370
And that's happening in Georgia.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:53.630
So people don't really know.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.989
Republicans don't really know whether they
are free to distance themselves from Donald

00:03:56.989 --> 00:03:57.989
Trump.

00:03:57.989 --> 00:04:01.760
Nobody is going to attack him, but they are
afraid to distance themselves.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.610
And so there's kind of a kerfuffle, right?

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.209
There's confusion, there's chaos.

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:10.380
There's dissent in Georgia, Republicans, we
see some of them disagreeing with each other

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.380
publicly.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.430
They're going in different directions in terms
of strategy.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.410
I think they are collapsing under the weight
of Donald Trump's ego.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:19.569
And I think that's going to have a huge effect
in January.

00:04:19.569 --> 00:04:24.630
And so but but as far as just like this idea
of them staying home as some kind of solidarity

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:29.569
with the claims it was rigged, only a few
percent need to do that for it to potentially

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:32.150
make a difference, given how close it's expected
to be.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:37.389
Right, because the special elections, the
the special election on January 5th, the turnout

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:39.199
is going to be, by definition, lower.

00:04:39.199 --> 00:04:40.199
Right.

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:41.199
It's a special election.

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:43.379
And so the margins are going to be that much
more important.

00:04:43.379 --> 00:04:48.319
So if they actually do manage to get like
one percent, even just one percent of Georgia's

00:04:48.319 --> 00:04:53.659
turnout, Republican turnout to stay home,
then that could be the margin of difference

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:55.349
here I've been having.

00:04:55.349 --> 00:04:59.719
Not really an argument, but kind of a disagreement
about strategy with some in my audience where

00:04:59.719 --> 00:05:04.939
last week I said, listen, for 10 years on
this program, I've been saying increase civic

00:05:04.939 --> 00:05:06.719
engagement.

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:11.349
Voting is is a step one, is only step one,
but it is step one.

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:12.349
Increase turnout.

00:05:12.349 --> 00:05:14.860
Overall, the left does better with more turnout.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:19.690
And so I would not feel OK saying, hey, Republicans,
you know, you really should stay home.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:23.460
You really should boycott this thing, because
I feel like at a higher level, it's not what

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.460
I'm about.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.930
Now, some people in my audience wrote to me
and they said, listen, David, if Democrats

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.409
take the Senate and Kamala Harris becomes
the tie breaking vote, things will get done

00:05:32.409 --> 00:05:34.430
that would not otherwise get done.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:35.860
The country will be better off.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:40.789
Isn't there some greater good in just this
one time encouraging Republicans not to vote

00:05:40.789 --> 00:05:43.129
even if you have to lie to them about the
rigged stuff?

00:05:43.129 --> 00:05:44.550
I can't get behind that.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.099
Like I'm having a lot of trouble with that.

00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:48.310
What's your thought on this idea?

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.310
No, no, no.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:53.539
It's a great question because I'm actually
I'm senior adviser for really American.

00:05:53.539 --> 00:05:58.500
And they are running some really provocative
ads in Georgia, billboards particularly.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:04.039
They have billboards all over the state that
are basically basically encouraging Republicans

00:06:04.039 --> 00:06:06.139
to continue fighting each other.

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:07.240
Right.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.770
And in a way, if you're not careful, it could
have a suppressive effect against Republicans,

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:16.830
because if Republicans feel like they have
a lot of infighting, they're more likely to

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:17.830
stay home.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:19.219
I'm OK with that.

00:06:19.219 --> 00:06:22.460
I and I 100 percent understand where you come
from, David.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.279
And I find myself struggling with that on
a regular basis.

00:06:25.279 --> 00:06:28.089
Well, let me clarify then, just to make sure
I know what you're saying.

00:06:28.089 --> 00:06:29.089
You're saying you're OK.

00:06:29.089 --> 00:06:30.599
I'm OK with them staying home.

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:33.499
If they want to convince each other to stay
home, that's great.

00:06:33.499 --> 00:06:35.589
I'm not going to say, you know, you really
should vote.

00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:40.289
Where I sort of draw the line is participating
in the encouragement to stay home because

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:41.569
it's rigged.

00:06:41.569 --> 00:06:46.680
What we're participating and what my colleague
at really American calls a truth telling campaign.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.029
OK, what's what's this been?

00:06:49.029 --> 00:06:50.159
It was a great spin.

00:06:50.159 --> 00:06:54.119
So he said all we're doing is telling the
truth about what's going on in the Georgia

00:06:54.119 --> 00:06:55.119
Republican Party.

00:06:55.119 --> 00:06:58.629
I think the people of Georgia have the right
to understand the divisions that are happening

00:06:58.629 --> 00:06:59.629
in the Republican Party.

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:02.409
So we're calling what we're doing a truth
telling campaign.

00:07:02.409 --> 00:07:06.259
And, you know, I know, you know, we could
read between the lines.

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.259
We want to win Georgia.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:11.991
And then you guys, you know, people who disagree
with me, not you particularly, but you could

00:07:11.991 --> 00:07:12.991
come after us afterwards.

00:07:12.991 --> 00:07:17.159
We're going to we're going to win this race
and and then and then we'll feel bad about

00:07:17.159 --> 00:07:18.740
what we did afterward.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:22.569
So you're confident about the two races?

00:07:22.569 --> 00:07:24.629
I'm pretty pretty bullish on it.

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:32.449
I feel I feel that if everything falls in
place, we could pull out a miracle.

00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:33.559
It will be a miracle.

00:07:33.559 --> 00:07:34.559
Right.

00:07:34.559 --> 00:07:36.939
It's not going to be easy, but I think all
the elements are in place.

00:07:36.939 --> 00:07:39.710
And I think if everyone plays their cards
right, we can get two seats.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:44.479
And I know that's a stretch, but I'm willing
to to to go out on the limb there.

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:49.649
Let's talk a little bit about the incoming
Biden administration.

00:07:49.649 --> 00:07:53.839
I said months ago, I will call it the way
I see it.

00:07:53.839 --> 00:07:57.629
If I see Joe Biden pick people that I think
are problematic, I will tell you it won't

00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:00.319
mean that I think I should have voted for
Trump instead.

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:02.179
It will just mean I voted for the right person.

00:08:02.179 --> 00:08:05.719
But they made some selections I didn't like
so far.

00:08:05.719 --> 00:08:10.039
The two that I am concerned about primarily
are Neera Tanden.

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:11.099
Absolutely.

00:08:11.099 --> 00:08:12.099
And Lloyd Austin.

00:08:12.099 --> 00:08:18.529
And, you know, back in twenty seventeen, I
said when Jim Mattis was selected, guys, we

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:21.819
have a rule about being out of the military
seven years.

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:25.819
So what's the point of the rule if you can
just get a congressional waiver and get around

00:08:25.819 --> 00:08:27.900
that there's a reason that that rule is in
place.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:32.459
The exact same waiver is going to be necessary
for Lloyd Austin, and I would be a hypocrite

00:08:32.459 --> 00:08:33.620
if I didn't call it out.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:38.399
He was involved in some of the mishandling
of of the Iraq war in 2011.

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:40.810
And in twenty fourteen, this this is not a
pic I love.

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:48.380
And to me, it strikes me as identity diversification
without solving the structural problems.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:49.380
Right.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:50.380
Right.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.819
Well, I mean, so there's always going to be
David, there's always going to be an element

00:08:52.819 --> 00:08:59.840
in the corporate Democratic wing that is more
than happy to give us identity, you know,

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.910
because it is it is a it is instantly celebrated
that this is a black guy who's going to lead

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:06.910
the Pentagon.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:07.910
Right?

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:08.910
Well, Lloyd Austin.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:09.910
Yep.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:13.399
But, you know, we just take a break and breathe
and look and say, oh, well, this is that guy.

00:09:13.399 --> 00:09:16.399
And so I think it's you know, that's our job.

00:09:16.399 --> 00:09:21.860
Our job is going to be to maneuver through
the both of them using identity as a shield,

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.829
like, I think identity politics matters.

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:28.460
I want to make sure I say that I think identity
politics matters because black people and

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:32.269
all kind of identities suffer identity specific
problems in this country.

00:09:32.269 --> 00:09:36.519
There are problems that black people go through
simply because they are black LGBTQ.

00:09:36.519 --> 00:09:42.329
I obviously, however, when the corporate Democrats
use it as a shield against criticism, we have

00:09:42.329 --> 00:09:46.970
to be able to maneuver through that and still
bring a solid critique against their their

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.350
corporate corporatism and imperialism.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:50.350
Right.

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:55.519
The phrase I've used is I value identity because
of the insight it gives.

00:09:55.519 --> 00:10:01.540
But where I stop is whether it's used as either
a shield or a cudgel or a mallet to beat people

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:05.990
sort of over the head, and that's where the
rational thinking seems to go out, right.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:07.470
Well, and I agree with you.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.250
I agree with you all the way to the point
where I think that there are some situations

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.550
in this country that require a mallet to the
head.

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:18.170
I think that there are some very stubborn
situations in this country that are so intransigent

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.170
and malignant.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.310
To be quite honest, racism is malignant in
this country.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:25.430
And if we need to go straight head to head
with it, then fine.

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:30.800
But we have to be able to understand when
it's time to recognize how identity politics,

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:36.329
just like any I mean, any other politics are
used against you for powerful people to continue

00:10:36.329 --> 00:10:38.040
getting their way into getting their agenda.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:41.150
And we just have to know when that's happening
with identity.

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:45.670
And interestingly, I mean, it's good you mention
that because oftentimes class politicking

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:51.550
is also used in ways that it's just not identified
as pernicious.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:55.650
And with the phrase identity politics is being
used.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:59.500
You rarely hear that said about class politics,
but it's used very frequently.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.500
Yeah, yeah.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.759
And it's class politics is an identity politics
that I get down with.

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:09.279
I'm like I'm down like four flat tires with
class politics as well as race, racial politics.

00:11:09.279 --> 00:11:10.709
But I agree with you, right?

00:11:10.709 --> 00:11:11.709
It is.

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:15.530
If you're not careful, there's an element
of of a class critique that itself can become

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:16.530
a cudgel.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:17.530
It could become a bludgeoning tool.

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:23.470
It could become a weaponized way to ignore
logic and reason.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:24.470
Right.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:27.690
So we just have to be careful that anything
that we really believe in can be weaponized

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:28.960
against us.

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:35.190
If we're if we if we give it a free pass and
not and we're not willing to critique it truthfully

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:36.820
and in good faith.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.880
So who do you like that Joe Biden has selected
so far?

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.660
Nobody, nobody.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:49.649
No, Nancy, I'm really trying to think of any
Sedrick I forget Sedgwick's last name, the

00:11:49.649 --> 00:11:54.230
congressman who had that brilliant exchange
with Matt Gates about his son.

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.649
I can't think of Sedgwick's last name, but
he's OK.

00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:01.550
I'm not I'm honestly, I'm not excited about
any of this because I am.

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:05.920
What I have decided to do with Joe Biden is
to let him make his play, let him make his

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:06.920
point.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:08.569
I can't control who he picks, no matter how
much commentary we give.

00:12:08.569 --> 00:12:10.170
We can't control who he picks.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:15.100
But we can decide what type of opponent we
have in the White House is the opponent that

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:19.230
we have to oppose with all of our energy or
is the opponent that we need to keep an eye

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.639
on because he made some bad picks and we need
to keep an eye on what they're going to do.

00:12:22.639 --> 00:12:24.430
I think he's going to fall into that latter
category.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:29.850
Yeah, I mean, I've said that I dug back into
some of Janet Yellen economic writing from

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:34.069
the 90s where she was like an academic economist.

00:12:34.069 --> 00:12:35.300
She's she's pretty brilliant.

